{% doc %}
  Renders a responsive grid of product cards.

  Shared by the collection template and the homepage so the two cannot drift.
  Styles live in assets/critical.css rather than a section `{% stylesheet %}`
  block, because a snippet cannot own one and duplicating the rules across
  both sections would be a maintenance trap.

  @param {object} products - A product list (e.g. collection.products).
  @param {boolean} [available_first] - Sold-out products last (a campaign's page).
  @param {number} [section_index] - The calling section's section.index. A snippet can't read `section` itself; nil when the position is unknown.
{% enddoc %}

{% comment %}
  Image loading (SHO-143, SHO-155). Without a `loading` argument, image_tag
  loads every card eagerly in the first three sections, so a 50-product
  collection fetched 50 images up front.

  Only the first card is eager. The grid is one column on a phone (cards are
  at least 16rem), so cards two and three start below the fold there, and
  loading them eagerly made them compete with the LCP image for a phone's
  bandwidth: Lighthouse CI's collection page requested three images at once,
  at High, Medium and Medium priority (2026-10-06). apps/web made the same
  change in SHO-148. On a wide screen the rest of the first row is lazy but in
  view, so it still loads straight after layout.

  The first card is the Largest Contentful Paint candidate, so it also gets
  fetchpriority high, but only when its section is first on the page. The
  caller passes section.index in, because a rendered snippet can't see
  `section`. It is nil in the theme editor and the Section Rendering API, and
  Shopify's guidance is that nil must fall to the safe side: eager, normal
  priority. So the lazy case is the positive comparison, as Shopify recommends.

  A grid in the fourth section or later is lazy throughout, matching
  image_tag's own default: it is below the fold, and eager cards there would
  compete with what is on screen. `section_index > 3` is false for nil, so the
  editor and Section Rendering API still get eager images.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  The live campaign's products come first (SHO-169 merchandising), as
  campaignFirst in packages/shopify does on web and the app: two passes, the
  campaign's then the rest, each in the list's own order. `position` counts
  across both, for the image loading below.
{% endcomment %}
{%- liquid
  capture live_handle
    render 'campaign-live-handle'
  endcapture
  assign live_handle = live_handle | strip
  if live_handle == blank
    assign passes = 'all'
  else
    assign passes = 'campaign,rest'
  endif
  comment
    On a campaign's own page every product is in it, so the order that
    matters there is buyable first, sold out after (availableFirst).
  endcomment
  if available_first
    assign passes = 'available,sold_out'
  endif
  assign passes = passes | split: ','
  assign position = 0
-%}

<ul class="product-grid" role="list">
  {%- for pass in passes -%}
    {%- for product in products -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign in_campaign = false
        if live_handle != blank and product.metafields.double_helix.campaign.value.system.handle == live_handle
          assign in_campaign = true
        endif
        if pass == 'campaign' and in_campaign == false
          continue
        endif
        if pass == 'rest' and in_campaign
          continue
        endif
        if pass == 'available' and product.available == false
          continue
        endif
        if pass == 'sold_out' and product.available
          continue
        endif
        assign position = position | plus: 1
        assign image_loading = 'eager'
        assign image_priority = 'auto'
        if position > 1 or section_index > 3
          assign image_loading = 'lazy'
        elsif position == 1 and section_index == 1
          assign image_priority = 'high'
        endif
      -%}
      {% render 'product-card', product: product, image_loading: image_loading, image_priority: image_priority %}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
</ul>
